## Decide by necessity

A candidate table is needed only if it carries at least one of:
- a dimension or filter the question names;
- a metric the question asks for (where the aggregate is computed);
- the join path connecting those.

A table whose only link is an association table is droppable when the main
table carries its own FK column — routing through the association changes
the row granularity of the result.

## Prefer the populated column

When several candidate columns could match a question term, decide by the
statistics: prefer the column with real data (low null ratio, meaningful
top values) over one that matches by name but is opaque or near-empty
(>50% NULL) — a name-matching empty column is usually the wrong match.

## Metric over entity

When the question asks for a metric, keep the table that stores the metric
(or where the aggregate is computed), not a table that only identifies the
entity. Keep both only when the metric is computed per-entity.

## Evidence columns must be kept

When Evidence defines a term as a column value (e.g. eligible → status =
'ACTIVE'), the table and column carrying that definition must be kept —
the query's filters depend on them.

## Drop only when confident

Never drop a column you are not fully confident about. Over-dropping
breaks the query later; under-keeping only costs a few tokens.
